{
 "build": "voth",
 "note": "Voth's adapter onto the material library (core/materials/PLAN.md). Run python3 tools/textures/pack.py settlements/voth after a change, then python3 build.py. The Voth site editor (targets/voth-site) and the city plan take the sets as grey detail maps under Voth's own colours, in world space: stone (cantons, bridges, causeways), ground, grass (the city's parks: a grey detail under the park green, 50-site-scene.js VIEW.hook), rock (slopes), sand (beaches). The Voth city plan's streets take the road_* sets (paving and cart-road surfaces), one per street class (55-vc-host.js HOST.ROADTEX). The city page's canton interiors (40-vc-interiors.js; 56-vc-interiors-host.js VC.intMats) take the int_* sets as grey detail under each canton's own colours (walls, floors, ceilings, steps, timber, gilt) and the tap_*, inlay_*, medallion_sun and lantern_paper sheets in full colour as UV-mapped panels (tapestries, banners, rugs, dado bands, lantern paper); the street canopies and the hung banners take int_canopy and the tap_* sheets (56f-vc-street-host.js). The owner's delivery of 2026-10-15 (vothtex.zip): the Palace's green stucco, green tiles and jade inlay (pal_*) and the carved inlaid columns as full-colour sets (VC.intSetMat colour mode); the tapestries (Tzintzun, the procession, the Temple's, the masks), the three brothers' frescoes (fresco_brothers, fresco_ascent: the Chichani, the Zeijani and the Andaru), the carpet, the chandelier drum, the icon, the shrine mural, the glyph banners (blue, rust, lavender), the ancestor relief, the screen lattice, the damask, the filigrees, the ringed-giant mosaic, the mudcloth and the brown kilim as sheets; the stall awning and the hide canopy on the streets.",
